% Number 85. Chace vol. II pl. 107 (photograph XXIX), PDF pp. 261-262; Peet p. 128 (Pl. X, bottom, left).
% Not a computation: a line of "cursive hieroglyphic", taken by Chace (after Gunn) as enigmatic writing.
% Peet gives no translation ("wiser not to follow Eisenlohr's example of attempting a translation";
% possibly a pen trial).
No. 85
"Interpret thou the strange thing [...] he, according to what knew he."   % Chace: a lacuna caused by a patch, not more than half as long as the surviving part of the line
